# GAIA 2.0 layered licensing

GAIA 2.0 does not use a single license. Layers have different licenses so
protocols stay unencumbered while implementation can take patents-friendly
or maximum-adoption terms.

| Tree | License | File |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `gaia-kernel/`, `gaia-sfs/`, `gaia-memos/` | Apache-2.0 | `LICENSE-APACHE-2.0` |
| `gaia-orchestrator/`, `gaia-agents/`, `gaia-interface/`, `gaia-sdk/`, `gaia-examples/` | MIT | `LICENSE-MIT` |
| `gaia-spec/`, `rfcs/` (protocol RFCs) | CC0 1.0 | `LICENSE-CC0` |
| `gaia-docs/` | CC-BY-4.0 | `LICENSE-CC-BY-4.0` |
| `Documents/`, `Documents-2/` | See each source document (blueprint v0.1 is CC0) | — |
| Root scaffolding (this file, CONTRIBUTING, templates) | Apache-2.0 unless a more specific file applies | `LICENSE-APACHE-2.0` |

Why not GPL: the blueprint (Part III) chooses Apache-2.0 for kernel-adjacent
code to keep enterprise adoption and explicit patent grants. Protocols are
CC0 so any implementation can interoperate.

Each layer directory also contains a `LICENSE` pointer file.
